// rate.mn — the course runs at 48,000 samples a second, said once
//
// The clock is the `Sample(48000)` effect (lib/dsp/clock.mn), and the rate
// is pinned HERE, at the three readers of the clock: `Sample(48000)` on each
// says which instance of the effect the course performs, and every lesson
// that calls them carries that instance in its projected row without writing
// it down. `play` is the course's render loop — one frame per tick until the
// clock reaches the end — and its `!Alloc` is a GATE on the frame it is
// handed (SYNTAX §«Negation in `with` clauses»): a lesson whose frame
// allocates refuses at the call, and the refusal names this declaration.
import dsp/clock

fn rate() with Sample(48000) = sample_rate()

fn now() with Sample(48000) = current_sample()

fn advance() with Sample(48000) = advance_sample()

// How many frames `s` seconds last at the installed rate.
fn frames_of(s: Int) = rate() * s

// The time, in seconds, the clock has reached.
fn time() = float_of_int(now()) / float_of_int(rate())

// The render loop: the frame is handed the index of the sample it renders,
// and the clock advances after it. Tail-recursive, so it is a loop in the
// emitted code.
fn play(frames: Int, frame) with !Alloc + !Sample(44100) = if now() >= frames {
  ()
} else {
  frame(now())
  advance()
  play(frames, frame)
}
